# Force LF line endings for shell scripts so bash (including
# git-bash on Windows) executes them without CRLF interpretation
# issues. Without this, `core.autocrlf=true` checkouts on Windows
# can turn `.sh` files into CRLF and bash mis-parses the shebang.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Patterns file is read line-by-line by publish-public-mirror.sh.
# Tolerated by the script's CRLF strip, but pinning LF avoids any
# surprise.
scripts/publish-scrub-patterns.txt text eol=lf

# Embedding-bits manifest's hash needs to be stable cross-platform.
# Pre-fix the file's sha256 differed between Windows-side commits
# (CRLF after `git add` normalization) and Linux-side checkouts (LF
# after extraction), which made the install_state.manifest_sha256
# row's cross-check value drift from the generator's reported hash.
# Pinning LF means the manifest hash on disk matches the value
# reported by ``scripts/build-embedding-bits-manifest.py`` regardless
# of who ran the generator.
scripts/embedding-bits-manifest.json text eol=lf

# COMMIT carries the build identifier baked into /app/VERSION (Containerfile
# `COPY COMMIT /app/VERSION`, read by app._resolve_version). `export-subst`
# makes `git archive HEAD` substitute the real commit hash ($Format:%H$ -> the
# 40-char SHA) at archive time, so EVERY archive-based build bakes the correct
# SHA — both `scripts/deploy.sh` (which also overwrites COMMIT) AND any manual
# `git archive HEAD | podman build`. Without this, manual builds shipped the
# literal "dev" placeholder and /health reported version "unknown". eol=lf so
# the substituted SHA is not followed by CRLF on Windows checkouts.
COMMIT text eol=lf export-subst
